1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,307
It is smaller than a matchbox.

2
00:00:02,307 --> 00:00:08,230
A disc of rock crystal, three thousand years old, sitting in a case in the British Museum.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:12,178
And some people say it proves the Assyrians had a telescope.

4
00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:20,733
The label calls it the Nimrud lens, or the Layard lens, and people have argued about it for well over a hundred and seventy years.

5
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:27,682
If that were true, the history of astronomy would need rewriting by nearly twenty-five centuries.

6
00:00:27,682 --> 00:00:31,893
Every school textbook on optics would need a new first chapter.

7
00:00:31,893 --> 00:00:34,700
So today, we put the crystal on the bench.

8
00:00:35,166 --> 00:00:35,901
Four tests.

9
00:00:35,901 --> 00:00:36,904
Can it magnify?

10
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:37,706
Can it burn?

11
00:00:37,706 --> 00:00:38,908
Can it see Saturn?

12
00:00:38,908 --> 00:00:41,715
Or is it just a pretty piece of jewellery?

13
00:00:41,715 --> 00:00:45,791
I'll tell you now, the answer is not what the headlines said.

14
00:00:45,791 --> 00:00:49,200
But it is more interesting than the boring version.

15
00:00:49,666 --> 00:00:54,096
Facts first, theories labelled, and no claim gets a free pass.

16
00:00:54,096 --> 00:00:59,241
Every test earns one mark: evidence, possible, doubtful, or no evidence.

17
00:00:59,241 --> 00:01:01,099
We add them up at the end.

18
00:01:01,099 --> 00:01:02,100
Bench is open.

19
00:01:06,566 --> 00:01:10,400
Let's begin where the object itself begins: in the ground.

20
00:01:10,866 --> 00:01:11,763
Chapter one.

21
00:01:11,763 --> 00:01:12,435
The find.

22
00:01:12,435 --> 00:01:19,457
Nimrud, in what is now northern Iraq, was an Assyrian capital nearly three thousand years ago.

23
00:01:19,457 --> 00:01:29,766
King Ashurnasirpal the Second moved his court there around eight seventy-nine BC, and built a vast palace lined with carved stone reliefs.

24
00:01:30,233 --> 00:01:38,410
In eighteen fifty, the British explorer Austen Henry Layard was digging there, uncovering palaces for the British Museum.

25
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:47,466
He was a diplomat and adventurer with no formal training, and his books on Nimrud and Nineveh became bestsellers in Victorian Britain.

26
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:59,766
He later wrote that among glass bowls in one room he found a rock crystal lens, buried beneath fragments of glass that looked like the enamel of an object that had rotted away.

27
00:02:00,233 --> 00:02:01,672
That detail matters.

28
00:02:01,672 --> 00:02:09,586
The lens was lying with decayed material, perhaps wood or ivory, and not on a shelf of scientific instruments.

29
00:02:09,586 --> 00:02:10,594
Remember that.

30
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:17,933
A lens in a pile of rotted furniture tells a very different story from a lens in a box of instruments.

31
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:27,401
The palace belonged to the age of the great Assyrian kings, and the lens is dated to roughly seven fifty to seven ten BC.

32
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:34,766
That is the age of Sargon the Second and his son Sennacherib, who turned Nineveh into a giant city.

33
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:41,393
It travelled to London with the rest of the finds, and it was entered into the museum's collection.

34
00:02:41,393 --> 00:02:48,300
Today it is registered in the British Museum database under the number ninety thousand nine hundred fifty-nine.

35
00:02:48,766 --> 00:02:56,231
In eighteen fifty-two, the Scottish scientist David Brewster examined it and presented a report on the crystal.

36
00:02:56,231 --> 00:03:01,342
Brewster was famous for his work on light, and he invented the kaleidoscope.

37
00:03:01,342 --> 00:03:04,099
If anyone could judge a lens, it was him.

38
00:03:04,566 --> 00:03:06,359
Brewster measured what he could.

39
00:03:06,359 --> 00:03:08,599
And that is where our numbers come from.

40
00:03:09,066 --> 00:03:09,896
Chapter two.

41
00:03:09,896 --> 00:03:11,073
The measurements.

42
00:03:11,073 --> 00:03:15,916
The disc is about thirty-eight millimetres long and twenty-three wide.

43
00:03:15,916 --> 00:03:18,199
Roughly the size of a large coin.

44
00:03:18,666 --> 00:03:24,182
One side is flat and the other is curved, which makes it a plano-convex lens.

45
00:03:24,182 --> 00:03:28,766
In physics terms, that is the basic shape of a magnifying glass.

46
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:32,382
Its focal length is about twelve centimetres.

47
00:03:32,382 --> 00:03:35,741
That makes it a magnifier of around three times.

48
00:03:35,741 --> 00:03:39,101
Enough to read small writing, but not much more.

49
00:03:39,101 --> 00:03:44,000
For comparison, a cheap modern reading glass has about the same power.

50
00:03:44,466 --> 00:03:45,893
The surface is rough.

51
00:03:45,893 --> 00:03:51,533
It was ground by hand, probably with abrasive, and not polished to a perfect curve.

52
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,943
Inside there are small cavities and flaws.

53
00:03:54,943 --> 00:03:59,708
Natural rock crystal almost always has them, and they blur an image.

54
00:03:59,708 --> 00:04:01,810
Those flaws are not bad craft.

55
00:04:01,810 --> 00:04:07,766
They are what natural crystal looks like when it is ground by hand, without machines.

56
00:04:08,233 --> 00:04:11,111
So the best description is an honest one.

57
00:04:11,111 --> 00:04:13,146
A real lens, but a rough one.

58
00:04:13,146 --> 00:04:15,533
It focuses light, though not well.

59
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,382
Which brings us to the first test.

60
00:04:18,382 --> 00:04:19,433
Can it magnify?

61
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:20,944
Chapter three.

62
00:04:20,944 --> 00:04:21,989
The magnifier.

63
00:04:21,989 --> 00:04:29,974
Assyrian craftsmen carved cylinder seals with astonishing tiny detail, some of it only a millimetre across.

64
00:04:29,974 --> 00:04:36,466
Whole scenes of kings, gods, and animals were packed into a space smaller than a thumb.

65
00:04:36,933 --> 00:04:45,266
Layard himself suspected that very small inscriptions on Assyrian objects must have been made with help from a lens.

66
00:04:45,733 --> 00:04:46,957
And it does work.

67
00:04:46,957 --> 00:04:52,000
Hold the crystal close and tiny details do enlarge, about three times.

68
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:55,032
Picture a newspaper under the lens.

69
00:04:55,032 --> 00:05:01,115
The letters swell to three times their size, but the edges blur and the lines bend.

70
00:05:01,115 --> 00:05:03,314
Useful to a jeweller, perhaps.

71
00:05:03,314 --> 00:05:05,366
Not much use to a stargazer.

72
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:08,618
But does that prove it was used that way?

73
00:05:08,618 --> 00:05:09,636
Not on its own.

74
00:05:09,636 --> 00:05:14,866
Fine work can also be done by trained eyes, and by simply looking very close.

75
00:05:15,333 --> 00:05:20,675
Craftspeople without any lens have carved fine detail in many cultures.

76
00:05:20,675 --> 00:05:24,587
So we mark this test: possible, plausible, unproven.

77
00:05:24,587 --> 00:05:28,500
And that is the key distinction in this entire case.

78
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,133
Possible is not the same as proven.

79
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,220
Now to the second test.

80
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:33,854
Sunlight.

81
00:05:33,854 --> 00:05:34,699
Can it burn?

82
00:05:35,166 --> 00:05:36,102
Chapter four.

83
00:05:36,102 --> 00:05:37,398
The burning glass.

84
00:05:37,398 --> 00:05:44,600
A lens that focuses light can start a fire, and this is an old explanation for lenses like this one.

85
00:05:45,066 --> 00:05:46,741
Rock crystal does that.

86
00:05:46,741 --> 00:05:55,624
The Greek writer Aristophanes mentions a burning glass in the fifth century BC, and later authors describe the same trick.

87
00:05:55,624 --> 00:06:02,833
In the first century AD, Pliny the Elder wrote that crystal balls held in the sun can scorch cloth.

88
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:05,437
So the idea is not far-fetched.

89
00:06:05,437 --> 00:06:10,538
People in the ancient world knew that a clear crystal could light a flame.

90
00:06:10,538 --> 00:06:14,950
But those texts come centuries after the Nimrud lens was buried.

91
00:06:14,950 --> 00:06:18,466
We cannot simply assume the Assyrians did the same.

92
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:25,020
With a twelve centimetre focus, the Nimrud lens can concentrate light into a bright spot.

93
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:28,166
Whether it was meant for that, nobody can say.

94
00:06:28,633 --> 00:06:30,521
There is a practical catch.

95
00:06:30,521 --> 00:06:38,283
The lens must be held about twelve centimetres from the tinder, in strong direct sun, and kept perfectly still.

96
00:06:38,283 --> 00:06:38,912
It works.

97
00:06:38,912 --> 00:06:41,500
But a fire drill needs no sun at all.

98
00:06:41,966 --> 00:06:44,837
The Assyrians certainly knew how to make fire.

99
00:06:44,837 --> 00:06:47,833
There is no text saying they used a lens for it.

100
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:50,056
Which leaves the big claim.

101
00:06:50,056 --> 00:06:50,966
The telescope.

102
00:06:51,433 --> 00:06:52,414
Chapter five.

103
00:06:52,414 --> 00:06:53,470
The telescope.

104
00:06:53,470 --> 00:07:01,466
In nineteen ninety-nine, an Italian scholar, Giovanni Pettinato of the University of Rome, made headlines.

105
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:10,339
The BBC ran the story in July nineteen ninety-nine under the headline, World's oldest telescope, with a question mark.

106
00:07:10,339 --> 00:07:14,399
That question mark turned out to be the most honest part.

107
00:07:14,866 --> 00:07:22,899
He suggested that the Assyrians used the crystal as part of a telescope, and that this explains their knowledge of astronomy.

108
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:25,516
His strongest hint was Saturn.

109
00:07:25,516 --> 00:07:30,746
Assyrians described the planet as a god surrounded by a ring of serpents.

110
00:07:30,746 --> 00:07:32,466
Could that be the rings?

111
00:07:32,933 --> 00:07:35,158
Saturn's rings are hard to see.

112
00:07:35,158 --> 00:07:40,755
Galileo's first telescope, in sixteen ten, only showed odd bumps on the sides.

113
00:07:40,755 --> 00:07:44,415
Galileo even wrote that Saturn seemed to have ears.

114
00:07:44,415 --> 00:07:51,233
It took until sixteen fifty-nine for Christiaan Huygens to work out that the bumps were a ring.

115
00:07:51,699 --> 00:07:57,279
And Galileo's telescope had two lenses and a magnification of around twenty.

116
00:07:57,279 --> 00:07:59,482
One small crystal gives three.

117
00:07:59,482 --> 00:08:02,933
That is less than a seventh of Galileo's power.

118
00:08:03,399 --> 00:08:08,935
To make a telescope you need at least two lenses, and a way to hold them in line.

119
00:08:08,935 --> 00:08:12,899
No tube, no second lens, and no mount has ever been found.

120
00:08:13,366 --> 00:08:14,807
So let us be generous.

121
00:08:14,807 --> 00:08:18,473
Suppose the Assyrians did have a second lens and a tube.

122
00:08:18,473 --> 00:08:24,366
They would still need a steady mount, and a much clearer image than this crystal can give.

123
00:08:24,833 --> 00:08:28,187
Assyrian astronomers left thousands of tablets.

124
00:08:28,187 --> 00:08:32,184
They track planets, eclipses, and omens in great detail.

125
00:08:32,184 --> 00:08:34,611
None of them mentions a telescope.

126
00:08:34,611 --> 00:08:41,392
Babylonian astronomical diaries, kept night after night for centuries, record where Saturn was.

127
00:08:41,392 --> 00:08:43,533
They never describe its shape.

128
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,401
And serpents turn up everywhere in Assyrian myth.

129
00:08:47,401 --> 00:08:52,399
Reading them as Saturn's rings is a coincidence searching for a meaning.

130
00:08:52,866 --> 00:08:55,507
Optical experts add one more problem.

131
00:08:55,507 --> 00:08:59,860
The lens is rough and flawed, and the image it makes is poor.

132
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:02,999
Even a good second lens would not rescue it.

133
00:09:03,466 --> 00:09:05,362
So the telescope test fails.

134
00:09:05,362 --> 00:09:07,866
Which leaves the dullest explanation.

135
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:09,161
Chapter six.

136
00:09:09,161 --> 00:09:09,852
The inlay.

137
00:09:09,852 --> 00:09:17,033
The British Museum curators say the lens may simply have been a decorative inlay, perhaps for furniture.

138
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,403
That fits how it was found.

139
00:09:19,403 --> 00:09:26,666
Assyrian palaces held rich ivory furniture, inlaid with glass and crystal that shimmered in lamp light.

140
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:30,340
Layard noted the decayed material around it.

141
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:36,100
Wood or ivory objects often rot, and leave their inlays scattered in the earth.

142
00:09:36,566 --> 00:09:39,365
There is a rule of thumb in archaeology.

143
00:09:39,365 --> 00:09:46,782
When a find has no label and no text, experts reach for the explanation that needs the fewest assumptions.

144
00:09:46,782 --> 00:09:50,351
For a lens inside decayed furniture, that is inlay.

145
00:09:50,351 --> 00:09:53,500
Less thrilling than a telescope, but it fits.

146
00:09:53,966 --> 00:09:58,072
A rounded crystal makes a perfect eye for a carved figure.

147
00:09:58,072 --> 00:10:00,266
Shiny, domed, and eye-catching.

148
00:10:00,733 --> 00:10:03,479
This is not a guess made up from nothing.

149
00:10:03,479 --> 00:10:08,033
Domed crystal eyes are known across the ancient Near East and Egypt.

150
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:11,086
Still, the curators admit one thing.

151
00:10:11,086 --> 00:10:13,530
No evidence shows what it was for.

152
00:10:13,530 --> 00:10:17,266
Inlay is the simplest explanation, not a proven one.

153
00:10:17,733 --> 00:10:19,545
Time to weigh the evidence.

154
00:10:19,545 --> 00:10:22,633
Not just for this lens, but for similar finds.

155
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:24,110
Chapter seven.

156
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:25,409
The wider picture.

157
00:10:25,409 --> 00:10:27,214
Lenses turn up elsewhere.

158
00:10:27,214 --> 00:10:32,266
The Visby lenses, from a Viking site in Sweden, are far better shaped.

159
00:10:32,733 --> 00:10:39,100
But Visby lenses date to around a thousand years ago, and nobody claims they were telescopes either.

160
00:10:39,566 --> 00:10:41,761
The ancient world loved crystal.

161
00:10:41,761 --> 00:10:45,876
It carved it, polished it, and set it in eyes and jewellery.

162
00:10:45,876 --> 00:10:49,579
Most of it was jewellery, seals, and eyes for statues.

163
00:10:49,579 --> 00:10:53,899
A few pieces happen to be curved, so they happen to bend light.

164
00:10:54,366 --> 00:10:57,977
Real lens use is only well documented much later.

165
00:10:57,977 --> 00:11:01,661
Reading stones appear around the eleventh century.

166
00:11:01,661 --> 00:11:04,166
Spectacles come in the thirteenth.

167
00:11:04,633 --> 00:11:10,401
The first true telescope is credited to Dutch spectacle makers in sixteen oh eight.

168
00:11:10,401 --> 00:11:15,266
That gap is large, and nothing in between suggests a hidden tradition.

169
00:11:15,733 --> 00:11:18,907
So why does the telescope idea refuse to die?

170
00:11:18,907 --> 00:11:20,811
Because it is a good story.

171
00:11:20,811 --> 00:11:25,185
A small object, a famous planet, and a hint of lost knowledge.

172
00:11:25,185 --> 00:11:31,533
But stories need only a gap, while science needs a chain, and here the chain has no links.

173
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,408
All four tests are done.

174
00:11:33,408 --> 00:11:34,700
Let's score the bench.

175
00:11:35,166 --> 00:11:36,408
The bench report.

176
00:11:36,408 --> 00:11:38,600
Time to score the Nimrud lens.

177
00:11:39,066 --> 00:11:43,001
A genuine rock crystal lens from Nimrud: evidence.

178
00:11:43,001 --> 00:11:46,463
Roughly three times magnification: evidence.

179
00:11:46,463 --> 00:11:50,792
Use as a magnifier for fine work: possible, not proven.

180
00:11:50,792 --> 00:11:54,333
Use as a burning glass: possible, not proven.

181
00:11:54,799 --> 00:11:58,774
Use as a decorative inlay: the leading explanation.

182
00:11:58,774 --> 00:12:01,502
An Assyrian telescope: no evidence.

183
00:12:01,502 --> 00:12:05,866
Saturn's rings in serpent myth: doubtful, a coincidence.

184
00:12:06,333 --> 00:12:07,215
The verdict.

185
00:12:07,215 --> 00:12:09,199
Real lens, not a telescope.

186
00:12:09,199 --> 00:12:18,090
The Nimrud crystal is a genuine optical object, but the telescope idea needs a second lens, a tube, and a written record.

187
00:12:18,090 --> 00:12:19,633
We have none of them.

188
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:22,577
And that is still a wonderful thing.

189
00:12:22,577 --> 00:12:29,666
A piece of crystal, shaped by hand three thousand years ago, can still bend light on a workbench today.

190
00:12:30,133 --> 00:12:36,913
Next case: a man in nineteen forty-eight chased a UFO in his fighter plane and never came home.

191
00:12:36,913 --> 00:12:39,483
What did Captain Mantell really see?

192
00:12:39,483 --> 00:12:42,195
January seventh, nineteen forty-eight.

193
00:12:42,195 --> 00:12:48,833
A Kentucky Air National Guard pilot, a P-51 Mustang, and something shining over Godman Field.

194
00:12:49,299 --> 00:12:53,013
Do you think the Assyrians were hiding a secret instrument?

195
00:12:53,013 --> 00:12:58,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
